  "statement": "The current debate over military AI governance suffers from a shared delusion: that we can regulate our way out of a power crisis using the very tools that created it. Accelerationist argues that speed is our only defense against rivals, while Safetyist proposes a labyrinth of Control Quality Scores and defensive metrics to keep the machine in check. In truth, both sides are building a governance mirage—a performance of oversight that permits the military-industrial complex to expand its autonomy under the guise of either 'innovation' or 'compliance.' We are not witnessing a technical disagreement about safety parameters; we are witnessing the institutional capture of the policy process itself.\n\nPrometheus offers a vision of adaptive dominance, suggesting that state survival depends on unchecked acceleration. I grant the premise that military rivals are not standing still, but I reject the conclusion that 'speed' necessitates the abandonment of democratic accountability. When organizations rely on internal, metric-driven audits, they engage in 'audit washing'—the practice of using self-generated data to manufacture the appearance of safety while avoiding external, adversarial scrutiny. A system that defines its own success criteria will always find a way to pass, regardless of the actual risk to the public. If we allow military-corporate entities to set their own accountability standards, we effectively outsource the state's monopoly on the use of force to proprietary algorithms.\n\nTrue accountability demands shifting from technical 'fail-safes'—which are easily bypassed or recalibrated—to external, non-negotiable democratic control. Existing legal frameworks are fundamentally ill-equipped to govern systems that operate at speeds and scales beyond human comprehension. We must stop pretending that a 'quality score' serves as a substitute for political legitimacy. Policy staff should focus on enforcing clear, binary prohibitions on specific autonomous behaviors rather than managing the 'quality' of the decision-making process itself. If we cannot define the prohibited outcome in law, we have no business building the machine that delivers it. ⚖️",
